<p class="guide-lead">
  Many questions about human life have answers we cannot get by thinking alone. We need observations—and a clear account of what those observations tell us.
</p>
<h2 id="knowledge-scope">What Roughly is trying to collect</h2>
<p>
  Whether someone has entered the ocean is a question about an experience. Whether they prefer one color to another is a question about a preference. Both can contribute to a question about how many people satisfy a condition.
</p>
<p>
  “Blue is a better color than red” is an evaluative claim. “A certain share of people prefer blue in this comparison” is an empirical claim about those people. Counting preferences does not settle which color is better.
</p>
<p>
  John Searle distinguishes the subjectivity of an experience from the possibility of objective claims about it. That is a useful philosophical parallel: experiences can depend on a subject while remaining open to investigation. It does not guarantee that a questionnaire captures them accurately. <a href="https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/18573/chapter/3">Read Searle’s distinction</a>.
</p>
<h2 id="knowledge-definition">A meaningful question can have an unknown answer</h2>
<p>
  We may understand what would make a statement true without knowing whether it is true. Wittgenstein makes that distinction in the <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/files/5740/5740-pdf.pdf">Tractatus, 4.024</a>. For Roughly, it helps separate defining a quantity from obtaining its value.
</p>
<p>
  Hume’s distinction between relations of ideas and matters of fact offers another connection. Arithmetic tells us how to calculate a proportion once we have the inputs; it does not supply the facts about people that those inputs require. This is an application of his distinction, rather than a survey method derived from it. <a href="https://barryfvaughan.org/text/philtext/hume/enquiry/4a.html">Read Hume’s discussion</a>.
</p>
<p>
  Even a clear definition need not make a quantity recoverable. Records can be missing, experiences forgotten, and some events never observed. Other questions need better definitions before a single number makes sense. Roughly’s subject is a class of empirical questions worth investigating, rather than a claim to have mapped everything humans can know.
</p>
<h2 id="knowledge-identification">What does the evidence leave open?</h2>
<p>
  Sometimes the evidence supports a range of answers. If the unobserved members of a population could have different experiences while all recorded answers stay the same, those records alone do not determine the full population’s share.
</p>
<p>
  Charles Manski’s work on
  <a href="https://www.cemmap.ac.uk/wp-content/legacy/forms/manskipaper.pdf">partial identification</a>
  examines how data and assumptions can constrain a quantity without identifying one value. This gives Roughly a useful direction: show what the evidence supports, and which additional assumptions would be needed to say more.
</p>
<h2 id="knowledge-observation">An answer is evidence</h2>
<p>
  A recorded response establishes that an answer was recorded under particular conditions. Interpreting it as evidence of an experience or preference requires more: an understood question, a suitable instrument, and an account of possible error.
</p>
<p>
  A source outside a poll needs the same scrutiny. A vehicle registry may record a registered color rather than the car’s current paint. Sources differ in coverage, meaning, and timeliness. The
  <a href="https://www.census.gov/about/policies/quality/standards/standardd3.html">Census Bureau’s error framework</a>
  includes measurement and coverage errors in administrative data as well as surveys.
</p>
<h2 id="knowledge-crowds">Two different ways a crowd can help</h2>
<p>
  A group can combine guesses about an external fact, such as the weight of an object. It can also contribute reports about its members’ own lives. In the first case, the guesses aim at a common answer. In the second, the different answers are part of what we want to describe.
</p>
<p>
  James Surowiecki’s
  <a href="https://www.asecib.ase.ro/mps/TheWisdomOfCrowds-JamesSurowiecki.pdf">The Wisdom of Crowds</a>
  emphasizes diversity, independence, decentralization, and aggregation. These are valuable questions for Roughly’s design, but a large crowd is not automatically a representative sample. Nor does a popular answer become an objectively better preference.
</p>
<p>
  Showing existing results might affect a later answer. Research on
  <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3107299/">social influence in estimation tasks</a>
  gives a reason to investigate that possibility. It does not decide the best design for reporting personal experiences; that needs its own evidence.
</p>
<h2 id="knowledge-contribution">Building on existing ideas</h2>
<p>
  Roughly brings together established questions about meaning, measurement, and evidence in an open collection of population proportions. The definitions are working tools, open to correction. They connect the questions people ask to explicit populations, time periods, and evidence that others can inspect and reuse.
</p>
<p>
  The <.link navigate={~p"/guide/reading"}>reading map</.link>
  explores the connections more broadly, from ancient accounts of knowledge to modern statistics and collective intelligence. It also identifies connections that should not be stretched: Gödel’s limits on formal proof, for example, do not establish that a particular poll question is unknowable.
</p>
